/** YD001 quality-pass translations siman 122 — hebrew_in_english cleanup */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '2#_': `They forbade. And in Mordechai they were lenient in siman 108 seif 3:`,
    '3#_': `Sharp. Issur VeHeter wrote: if one cooked a sharp food in a meat pot that is not ben yomo, and afterward cooked milk in it within twenty-four hours, the food is permitted, and we do not say that the sharpness in the pot causes it to emit now for improvement; and the same applies if one cooked a sharp food in a pot of issur that is not ben yomo (and Nachalat Binyamin emends that one must say it is ben yomo — see there), and afterward it stood from time to time since the sharp food was cooked in it — permitted; and likewise if one cooked a sharp food in a meat pot ben yomo, if one cooked in it afterward within twenty-four hours since the meat was cooked, even though it is not from time to time from when the sharp food was cooked, and when the sharp food was cooked in it it was ben yomo — permitted; but if issur was first cooked in it, even if afterward a non-sharp food was cooked, if it was ben yomo at that time, we count afterward from time to time from the time of the second dish, as above siman 94; and see siman 103 s.k. 7 in hagahah regarding heating water in it — see there:`,
    '5#_': `Small amount. In siman 99 s.k. 7 we explained that for the halachah we do not hold thus (except regarding spoiled issur) — see there, Taz:`,
    '6#א': `Permitted. The reason the poskim wrote is that it constitutes a safek sefeika: a doubt whether they used it today or not, and lest they used it with something that spoils visibly or that is not noten ta'am; (and accordingly the same applies to our vessels — there is safek sefeika and we rely to be lenient, and so too in Shulchan Aruch seif 7). And for this reason it is forbidden l'chatchila to cook in a utensil of a non-Jew, for there is only one doubt, that a pot that is not ben yomo is forbidden l'chatchila; and Maharshal wrote: this that stam utensils of gentiles are not ben yomo means one need not ask whether it is ben yomo or not; and if after asking it became known that it is ben yomo, one should ask what was cooked in it today, and if it is something that certainly does not spoil, or that he cannot know — one should not permit, since there is no safek sefeika here; and even though we are not concerned for the words of a non-Jew neither to forbid nor to permit — that is regarding something that was already in a presumption of kashrut — but here, since the presumption of stam utensils of a non-Jew has already been impaired, for on the contrary they absorbed from issur only through safek sefeika it came to permit, therefore we rely on his word; but from responsum of R' Levi ibn Chaviv siman 121 it appears explained that even if he says it is ben yomo we do not rely on his word (nevertheless that is specifically if he lent it and the dish was already cooked in it; but if he says while it is still in his hand, or after he lent it before it was cooked in it — even if one transgressed and cooked, it is forbidden; and see Peri Chadash s.k. 4; and see responsum of Mahari ben Lev vol. 1 daf 134 and vol. 2 siman 68); and Taz also ruled thus unless the non-Jew speaks of his own accord without any question, and it appears the matters were not directed at a Jew — then one should be stringent and forbid the dish of a Jew who cooks in it, as he wrote in siman 113 seif 2; and one may further discuss this as he wrote in siman 16, that if the non-Jew is believed to him it is forbidden — so too one may discuss here even if he does not speak of his own accord — end of his words:`,
    '6#ב': `For drinking. Shach wrote: it appears that if water was heated for drinking it is permitted to drink it, for regarding this it is called b'dieved (and permitted like other things taken from a non-Jew that are permitted since they were already made by the hand of the non-Jew); and see below siman 114 s.k. 7:`,
    '9#א': `An hour. Behold, Bach challenged from what he wrote below siman 136, that Tur and poskim hold that one who leaves a wine utensil in the hand of a non-Jew, even one hour, requires kashering, etc.; and he strained greatly, and the conclusion of his words is that for the halachah there too it is permitted to leave them from time to time and to use them l'chatchila — end of his words; and Shach wrote on this that he did not descend to the end of his intent, that with a wine utensil it is not relevant to distinguish between ben yomo and not ben yomo, for as long as one night has not passed upon them there is in them ta'am for improvement; and see there where he expanded on this matter and concluded that such is primary as he wrote here in hagahah, that if one placed it in the hand of a non-Jew from time to time it is permitted to use it l'chatchila; and all this is with utensils whose manner is to use them hot; but utensils whose manner is to use them cold — certainly rinsing suffices — end of his words; and Taz distinguishes that the essence depends on the matter whose manner is to use it, even occasionally, such as a wine utensil that a person is apt to drink from at any hour, and one should be concerned that in the short time it was with the non-Jew lest he drank wine from it — therefore they were stringent in siman 136 even for a short time; unlike a utensil in which they cook, or an eating knife, whose manner is not to use except at mealtime, and a person's manner is not to eat except a fixed measure in a day — therefore one should not be stringent except when it stood, like half a day; and some say not half a day but a large measure that one can estimate that in that time he used it for eating — end of his words:`,
    '9#ב': `B'dieved. Shach wrote: the food that was used in it — it is obvious it is permitted; rather Rama comes to teach us that since they already placed it in the hand of a non-Jew and took it from him, we are not concerned b'dieved and it is permitted to use it even l'chatchila — to exclude giving it to a non-Jew initially with intent that he leave it afterward from time to time — that is forbidden; and Taz disagrees with this hagahah and expanded greatly on this matter, and the conclusion of his words for the general rule is: where there is safek sefeika, such as that they did not return it the same day — if one placed it intentionally one should forbid the utensil and it requires hag'alah, and an earthenware utensil requires breaking; and if one forgot, there is room to permit the utensil, only if it is a utensil that can be kashered one kasheres it, and an earthenware utensil is permitted; but where there is no safek sefeika, such as that they returned it the same day — one should forbid even b'dieved by law; and that is: if it is an eating utensil, the issur is only that it should stand with the non-Jew part of the day, a large measure that according to estimation of the mind he used it for eating issur; and if it is a drinking utensil, like that of a wine utensil siman 136 that is forbidden l'chatchila, even for an hour — end of his words:`,
    '9#ג': `And maidservants. And people are not careful about this now; and the reason is because the servants and maidservants are in a Jew's house always, that a Jew goes in and out there — in such a case it is permitted even l'chatchila, as above siman 118 seif 10 — end of his words, Shach:`,
    '11#_': `To purchase them. Shach wrote: it appears to me the reason is that since stam keilim of a non-Jew are not ben yomo, if so there is only a rabbinic issur, and a non-Jew who speaks of his own accord is believed regarding a rabbinic issur:`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '2#_': `I also noted there in seif 5:`,
    '4#_': `I also noted there in seif 6:`,
    '5#_': `I noted it above in siman 99 seif 7:`,
    '6#א': `Agreed among the poskim except Rambam, from that in Avodah Zarah daf 75, etc.; all of them that were used in them, etc. — permitted according to one who says noten ta'am lifgam is permitted; and from that which we say regarding the cup of a non-Jew and regarding oil of Arameans, etc. — if because of purging of a non-Jew, noten ta'am lifgam is permitted; and Tosafot explained there: because it is a safek sefeika — a doubt whether they used it today or yesterday, and if you say they used it today, lest they used it with something that spoils or that is not noten ta'am; and so too Tur in the name of Rosh:`,
    '6#ב': `Tur in the name of Rashba in Teshuvot HaRosh, from that in Chullin daf 6, as we say: since he said to her "make from my dough," it is as one who kneads with his hands:`,
    '7#_': `Tur for the reason mentioned above (and siman 94 in the name of Ran, and so too all the acharonim, and not like Derishah siman 10 seif 5):`,
    '8#_': `I noted it above in siman 102, and there the reason is explained:`,
    '9#_': `Mordechai in chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah:`,
    '11#_': `Orach Chayim in the name of Rashba siman 457 in his view:`,
    '12#_': `Beit Yosef in the name of Orach Chayim and Kol Bo in the name of Raavad:`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#_': `Noten ta'am lifgam, etc. Until the end of seif 4 everything was explained correctly in siman 103 — see there:`,
    '3#_': `But if the issur was a sharp item, etc. In Darkei Moshe and in Turei Chayim general rule 61 law 12 he wrote in the name of Issur VeHeter: if one cooked a sharp food in a meat pot that is not ben yomo, and afterward cooked milk in it ( ) (after) [within] twenty-four hours, the food is permitted, and we do not say that the sharpness in the pot causes it to emit now for improvement — until here; and the same applies if one cooked a sharp food in a pot of issur that is not ben yomo, and afterward it stood from time to time since the sharp food was cooked in it — permitted; and likewise if one cooked a sharp food in a meat pot ben yomo, if one cooked in it afterward (thus) within twenty-four hours since the meat was cooked, even though it is not from time to time from when the sharp food was cooked, and it was ben yomo when the sharp food was cooked in it — permitted; but if issur was first cooked in it, even if afterward a non-sharp food was cooked, if it is ben yomo we count afterward from time to time from the cooking of the second dish, as above siman 94 s.k. 22:`,
    '5#_': `Behold this is permitted, etc. Ra'ah and Tur challenged on this, and likewise Bach — see there:`,
    '6#א': `["Stam, etc. The reason the poskim wrote is that it constitutes a safek sefeika: a doubt whether they used it today or yesterday, and if you say they used it today, lest they used it with something that spoils visibly or that is not noten ta'am; and for this reason it is forbidden to cook l'chatchila in utensils of gentiles, for there is only one doubt, that a pot that is not ben yomo is forbidden l'chatchila; and Maharshal wrote in his Avodat HaTaharah siman 59: this that stam utensils of a non-Jew are not ben yomo means one need not ask whether it is ben yomo or not; and if after asking it became known that it is ben yomo, one should ask what was cooked in it today, and if it is something that certainly does not spoil, or that he cannot know — one should not permit, since there is no safek sefeika here; and even though we are not concerned for the words of a non-Jew neither to forbid nor to permit (and see below siman 118 end of s.k. 37) — that is regarding that thing that was already in a presumption of kashrut; but here, since the presumption that stam utensils of a non-Jew are absorbed with issur has already been impaired, only through safek sefeika it came to permit — therefore we rely on his word; and furthermore this that we do not rely on the words of gentiles is where he comes to improve his merchandise that he said they are of orlah, as is stated in the Gemara — end of his words; but from responsum of R' Levi ibn Chaviv siman 121 it appears explained that even if he says it is ben yomo we do not rely on his word; and see below siman 127 s.k. (19) [20]:"]`,
    '6#ב': `["That were not heated for drinking, etc. It appears: but if they were heated for drinking it is permitted to drink them, for regarding this it is called b'dieved and permitted like other things taken from gentiles that are permitted since they were already made by the hand of the non-Jew — it is called b'dieved — end of his words, Turei Chayim beginning of general rule 59; and see below siman 114 seif 7; and see below siman 94 s.k. 19 and siman 95 s.k. 14:"]`,
    '6#ג': `["It is called b'dieved. See below siman 118 seif 1 in hagahah:"]`,
    '8#_': `A utensil that became forbidden, etc. Repeated above siman 102 seif 3, and there it was explained — see there:`,
    '9#א': `["And we are not concerned b'dieved. It does not mean that the food that was used in it is permitted b'dieved — that is obvious; and furthermore he has not yet mentioned anything of the food — rather it means that since they already placed it in the hand of a non-Jew and took it from him, we are not concerned b'dieved and it is permitted to use it even l'chatchila, as Ra'ah wrote and he brings it in Turei Chayim general rule 59 law 4; and so too in the simanim of Turei Chayim there — to exclude that l'chatchila it is forbidden to give it to a non-Jew with intent that he leave it afterward from time to time; and even though Ra'ah spoke only when the Jew left it from time to time it is permitted to use l'chatchila, it appears to the Rav that all the more so whenever we can say it stood in the hand of a non-Jew it is permitted, for it is not comparable to a utensil of a non-Jew; and so it appears in the simanim of Chavot Yair there; and even though in Turei Chayim there it appears that for Ra'ah, if one used it the same day without hag'alah the food is permitted — that is according to the Rav's view; but Issur VeHeter who holds the food is forbidden, and like the plain meaning of Mordechai's words, one may say that both exist: even though the food is forbidden — that is because one used it the same day — but whatever stood from time to time, even l'chatchila, is permitted; and for this the Rav wrote in hagahah here at the beginning in stam that we are not concerned, and that is when it stood from time to time that it is permitted to use l'chatchila; and afterward he wrote the view of those who forbid the food; and even though in Turei Chayim there it appears that for Issur VeHeter who forbids the food, he also forbids using the utensil even if it stood from time to time — nevertheless for the halachah it appears to the Rav to distinguish as he wrote; and all this according to the Rav; but Bach in this siman and in siman 137 disagrees and wrote: where it stood in the hand of a non-Jew half a day and he returned it the same day — it requires hag'alah, and if it is earthenware it requires breaking, and standing from time to time does not help; and if one used it the same day the food is forbidden — and this is like the view of Issur VeHeter who explained thus Mordechai's words in the name of Rabbeinu Tam; and where it stood in the hand of a non-Jew for an hour — standing helps to use l'chatchila, and in this the Orach Chayim in the name of Ra'ah deals; and if one used it without standing the food is permitted — and that is what Mordechai wrote in the name of Rabbeinu Tam; but for an hour one need not be concerned — end of his words; however what Bach challenged below siman 137 from what Tur and Rashi and the poskim wrote, that one who sends a wine utensil in the hand of a non-Jew even one hour requires kashering, etc., and he strained greatly, and the conclusion of his words is that Rabbeinu Tam in Mordechai does not hold like Tur and his companions; and for the halachah there too it is permitted to leave them from time to time and to use them l'chatchila; and where it stood in the hand of a non-Jew a day or two days it is forbidden to use them l'chatchila in any case, like utensils of a non-Jew themselves — and not like R' Shimshon of Bunberg in Mordechai who is alone against Tur and Rashi and the poskim who require kashering for a wine utensil; and for Rabbeinu Tam and Ra'ah in a cauldron whose use is hot he agrees that kashering is required — and not like Beit Yosef wrote in this siman 122 that Rabbeinu Tam and R' Shimshon of Bunberg agree on this matter — end of his words; and I did not descend to the end of his intent, for there with a wine utensil standing from time to time is not relevant, as we hold below siman 137 that a wine utensil is not subject to distinguishing between ben yomo and not ben yomo (and so too Ran at the end of Avodah Zarah); and as Ra'ash proved in responsum general rule 19 seif 96 that as long as twelve months have not passed upon them there is in them ta'am for improvement — therefore kashering is required for a wine utensil for Rashi and Tur and their companions, for Rabbeinu Tam's reason does not apply to them, that even if one used them it would be spoiled after from time to time; but in a cauldron everyone agrees to R' Shimshon of Bunberg that it is permitted to use them l'chatchila immediately, for the reason Maharshal wrote there: establish the cauldron on its presumption (and for this reason some say for utensils of gentiles that are forbidden to use l'chatchila — for a utensil of a Jew we say establish it on its presumption); and say it did not use issur, and furthermore even if it used it would be spoiled that is not ben yomo, for stam utensils of a non-Jew we establish them thus, as Rabbeinu Tam's words, and it would be a doubt of rabbinic issur and to be lenient — end of his words; and from now there is no dispute among the poskim at all, and this is clear; and so in Mordechai Perek Elu Metziot he brought Rabbeinu Tam's words and R' Shimshon of Bunberg's words in stam one after the other; and so Issur VeHeter general rule 33 ruled like both of them in stam — and this is the Rav's view in the simanim of Turei Chayim there; and here in hagahah, if one placed it in the hand of a non-Jew from time to time it is permitted to use l'chatchila — and so is primary; and all this is with utensils whose manner is to use them hot; but utensils whose manner is to use them cold — certainly rinsing suffices — investigate:"]`,
    '9#ב': `["And l'chatchila one should be careful in every case, etc. And people are not careful about this now, and the reason is because the servants and maidservants are in a Jew's house always, that a Jew goes in and out there — in such a case it is permitted even l'chatchila, as above siman 118 seif 10:"]`,
    '11#_': `Permitted to purchase them. It appears to me the reason is that since stam utensils of a non-Jew are not ben yomo, if so there is only a rabbinic issur, and a non-Jew who speaks of his own accord is believed regarding a rabbinic issur, as he wrote in siman 98 s.k. 2 — see there; and see below siman 121 s.k. 15:`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 122 in Shulchan Aruch seif 2) "And even so the Sages forbade to cook in it l'chatchila, etc." Rashba proved this law from the Gemara in Pesachim daf 39: R. Yehudah said, pots on Pesach should be broken, etc. — see there; and it is impossible that l'chatchila it is permitted to use a pot that is not ben yomo — why should they break them, leave them until after from time to time and use them on Pesach? Rather l'chatchila is forbidden; and one may say: Rashba holds that wherever issur is b'mashehu, nat bar noten ta'am is also forbidden even b'dieved; if so there is no proof from chametz on Pesach that forbids b'mashehu — if so nat bar noten ta'am is also forbidden even b'dieved; unlike other issurim — one may say that a pot that is not ben yomo is permitted to use l'chatchila; and one may say his proof is from Shmuel who holds not in its kind is batel in sixty even for chametz on Pesach — and this is certainly that even though Rashba holds to forbid nat bar noten ta'am in issurim of b'mashehu and the like, but where not in its kind is in sixty, even if mixed in its kind, and it is nat bar noten ta'am — it does not forbid, for this is certainly since it is lifgam there is no importance of its kind here; if so Shmuel holds not in its kind in sixty — if so it is impossible that in all issurim it is permitted to use l'chatchila — why should it be permitted to use chametz on Pesach, whether not in its kind or not in its kind? For all noten ta'am lifgam is not in its kind — and why did Shmuel say leave them until after Pesach and make, etc.? Rather certainly nat bar noten ta'am is forbidden l'chatchila:`,
    '2#_': `(There in Shach s.k. 2) "And in Darkei Moshe, etc., he wrote in the name of Orach Chayim: if one cooked a sharp food in a meat pot, etc." See there at length. And behold at first glance one should wonder at Rav Shach from this that we hold in siman 96 seif 1 that if one cut a radish with a meat knife that is not ben yomo, even though it is well scraped, and cooked it in milk — sixty is required against what is opposite the knife, and the reason is because of its sharpness of radish that renders it for improvement, and also it is not noten taam bar noten taam — if so here why do we not consider that when the sharp food was cooked in it the taste of the meat became for improvement, and there are not yet twenty-four hours from this cooking? And it appears to resolve: behold the teachers of Rashba wish to prove and say that this which we hold noten taam bar noten taam of heter is permitted — that is even if the second taste is in issur, not specifically fish that went into a bowl, but the same applies if they made kutach in the bowl of the meat itself — then immediately when the taste emits into the meat it is in issur, nevertheless it is permitted, for he holds that this which we hold ta'am ke'ikar is specifically when the taste was absorbed from the meat itself or from the milk itself; but when the taste was absorbed from what was absorbed in the pot in such a case we do not say ta'am ke'ikar at all d'oraisa; and behold all this is if the absorbed is in the pot and the like; unlike if the absorbed is in the food — for the taste entered the food and the absorbed is the first taste and became nevelah, and then what emits from it becomes issur within the food and forbids everything; unlike if the absorbed is in the utensil — for we hold that absorbed in a utensil is not noten noten ta'am, as the poskim wrote in several places — if so one should not forbid except after it emits from the utensil, for then it is a second taste that is not forbidden at all; and from now we have clarified that above in cold, that even according to the teachers of Rashba it is properly forbidden — the Mechaber closes to forbid; unlike here where otherwise there are views among the poskim whether it is called a sharp item, since it is not called a sharp item at all except a kort of chalitit, and according to the teachers of Rashba there is no forbidding at all — if so combining two views Shach relied to permit in such a case — investigate:`,
    '3#_': `(There seif 9 in hagahah) "And even if one gave them to a craftsman, etc." See Taz s.k. 8 and Shach s.k. 8 who expanded on this; and the ultimate intent is that they wish to explain Rama's words: if he returned them immediately it is forbidden because there is only one doubt; unlike if he returned them afterward from time to time — then there is safek sefeika lest he used before from time to time; and this is against reason, for certainly this which we hold regarding a utensil of a non-Jew that there is safek sefeika lest he did not use from time to time — that is that the entire essence of the doubt is lest he will not use at all, only since we see the utensil is old he certainly used it, we say regarding it that he did not use within from time to time; unlike here with eating utensils that they were concerned and decided that the non-Jew certainly used it — there is no room to say any doubt lest he did not use it; if so even if he did not return it immediately why should it be permitted, lest one hour before he returned it the non-Jew used it? Therefore it appears to me to explain that certainly Ran in the name of Ra'ah who was stringent with a knife — that is because the manner of a craftsman of gentiles when they give him a Jew's knife to use even though he gains nothing — in other matters we are not concerned for substitution in such a case; nevertheless here with a knife, since it is found in his hand and he needs a knife to use, he does not trouble after another knife and uses the Jew's; but knives that servants carry in a Jew's house — since he is in a Jew's house there is no concern here at all, and it is like a utensil of a non-Jew that is permitted through safek sefeika lest he did not use at all; and if you say he used, lest he cut with it something that spoils visibly — and this that Ritva wrote "and all this when they wish to use it on the day they gave it to him" — that is on the day the non-Jew returned it, even though it stood in the hand of a non-Jew many days — nevertheless there is concern lest he used it one moment before they give it to him; but if they took it immediately from the non-Jew until after a time when we can say even though the non-Jew used it, it is already spoiled — meaning one knows he did not use it in that from time to time, or it stood in the hand of a Jew after taking it from the non-Jew from time to time — we follow the lenient view because it is a rabbinic safek and to be lenient, and not because it is safek sefeika that there is no safek sefeika here at all, as he wrote; and this that R' Shimshon of Bunberg in Mordechai wrote regarding one who forgot a Jew's cauldron in the hand of a non-Jew — permitted for the reason of safek sefeika, and wrote even if used it would be spoiled that is not ben yomo, and stam utensils we establish thus — behold he holds that we say since it stood in the house a time when it could be, it is not ben yomo, we are not concerned lest he used it one moment before they returned it; one may say it deals with that he returned it to him immediately the next day, and at night there is no manner to use at all — if so one may say R' Shimshon holds that a night's standing spoils, and if so there is no room for concern at all; and so one may infer the language of the questioner who wrote that one forgot the cauldron in the house of a non-Jew until the next day — it appears he took it immediately the next day — investigate:`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 122 in Shach s.k. 2) (hagahah) What the Gaon Maharach wrote: "and the same applies if one cooked a sharp food in a pot of issur that is not ben yomo, and afterward it stood from time to time since the sharp food was cooked in it — permitted." I did not descend to the end of his intent — is this not all the more so from the law of Issur VeHeter? And furthermore, even if one cooked a sharp item in ben yomo and it stood from time to time — permitted, and even after another sharp issur from time to time — permitted, as the Rav wrote in hagahah; and it is possible there is a textual error and one must delete the word "that is not" and it should read "pot of issur ben yomo." Also in what the Gaon Maharach wrote: "and likewise if one cooked a sharp item in a meat pot ben yomo, if one cooked in it afterward from time to time since the meat was cooked in it, etc. — permitted," and he brings proof from siman 94 s.k. 22 — I am puzzled by this law, for it is different there where they did not cook a sharp food, unlike here where the second cooking is a sharp item and renders what is inside the walls of the utensil as if cooked now; but if one cooked a sharp item after from time to time, since its taste is spoiled it does not render for improvement as if cooked now, only for the sharp food itself and not for a second food; unlike the law if a sharp item was cooked within from time to time that its taste was not spoiled — one may say we consider as if the meat was cooked now; and so it appears proven from Issur VeHeter's words that he brings Orach Chayim — he should have written regarding a meat pot that is not ben yomo, he should have written a novelty even for ben yomo; rather certainly as I wrote — investigate:`,
    '2#_': `(There in Taz s.k. 8) "And furthermore Ra'ah's words are puzzling, for behold with a utensil of a non-Jew it is also permitted b'dieved, etc." His words are puzzling, for Ra'ah holds exactly like R' Shimshon of Bunberg — therefore some say for a utensil of a non-Jew: here, since it is a Jew's utensil, we say establish it on its presumption, as R' Shimshon wrote; and so too for R' Shimshon, if he returned them the same day — standing is required; only R' Shimshon deals there where it already stood in the hand of a non-Jew and he returned it the next day — therefore it is permitted immediately; and also his other words that he wrote here are not necessary at all; and see what Shach wrote on this:`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 122 in Be'er HaGolah letter 6) Agreed among the poskim except Rambam. From Rashi's words in Yoma (daf 1) it appears he agrees with Rambam's view — and see in Sefer Taharat Kohanim there:`,
    '2#_': `(Taz s.k. 4) Even when he does not speak casually. In Magen Avraham (Orach Chayim s.k. 10) he wondered at this, for Taz contradicts his own words above (siman 45 s.k. 19):`,
    '3#_': `(Shach s.k. 4) That it constitutes safek sefeika. One doubt permits more than its fellow, as Shach wrote in the laws of safek sefeika (law 12):`,
    '4#_': `(Seif 6 in hagahah) That they were not heated for drinking. See in Pri Megadim above (siman 94 in Sha'ar HaTaharah s.k. 19) who raised that in cooking water in a Jew's utensil that is ben yomo, even if heated for drinking it is forbidden — see there:`,
    '5#_': `(Seif 9 in hagahah) That we can say even if used. And regarding finding a knife in a Jewish street — see in responsum Shevut Yaakov (vol. 1 siman 47) and in Mishnat Chachamim (siman 63 letter 2):`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `That they were not heated for drinking. Avodat HaTaharah and in responsum Noda BiYehudah siman 36: cups called "schellin" that are in the Kaiserhof of gentiles — one should forbid drinking from them, for certainly they are ben yomo, for at every hour they drink milk issur from them; and one should not rely on the leniency of kli sheni either, for Rashal and Taz are stringent and Shach is stringent with earthenware l'chatchila; therefore in a city where there are Jewish utensils it is forbidden l'chatchila, and if one pours from kli rishon even b'dieved it is forbidden; and on the road in an inn where there are no other utensils it is considered b'dieved, and in a city one should be stringent even where impure milk is not common; and one who forgets in his hand to separate from issur performs a mitzvah — see there; and requires study, for behold all drinking in schellin is through pouring from kli rishon; and if so even on the road in an inn one should be stringent and pour from kli rishon to another utensil that is not accustomed to use every day, as we say in Yoreh De'ah stam utensils are not ben yomo, and from there one may pour to the schellin even if the hand is still hot; and if there is no hiddur it is all permitted even in a city, and through pouring, for presumably the utensils are clean and rinsed:`,
    '2#_': `And we are not concerned b'dieved. Avodat HaTaharah and in responsum Bach siman 109: an eating utensil whose use is hot in the hand of a non-Jew a day or two days — its law is like other utensils of gentiles and requires hag'alah; and if one used it b'dieved the food was not forbidden; and if it did not stand in the hand of a non-Jew except half a day and a Jew took it from him to use the same day — the food is forbidden even b'dieved; but if it stood in his hand from time to time then the food is permitted b'dieved; but it is forbidden to cook in it ever, rather it requires hag'alah and likewise breaking; and if it did not stand in the hand of a non-Jew except for an hour like part of the day — then a Jew leaves it from time to time and it is permitted to use even l'chatchila and one need not kasher; but it is forbidden to use within from time to time; and if one used within from time to time (through) the dish — permitted; and there are further distinctions in responsum of Rosh and Turei Chayim general rule 59 — see there:`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `L'chatchila. See in Minchat Yaakov general rule 85 s.k. 64: he brought that in responsum Nachalat Yaakov he wrote in the name of the sages of Venice that this is specifically with metal utensils, since there is remedy through hag'alah; unlike earthenware utensils that require breaking — it is b'dieved and they permit the utensils after standing from time to time; and he expanded to challenge this from several sugyot of the Gemara and the poskim; and nevertheless he concludes there in the name of one great one that in case of great loss one may permit in all cases — see there; and in my humble opinion it is difficult to permit even in case of great loss, for Hagahot Asheri at the end of tractate Avodah Zarah on that which says a decree because of a ben yomo pot — and these are his words: but one should not say the reason is because it is l'chatchila and noten ta'am lifgam is forbidden l'chatchila, for since they require breaking it is b'dieved — end of his words; behold explicitly that even in all cases breaking is required; I further found in Pri Megadim siman 103 who noted this as well; and see Pri Megadim in Orach Chayim in the conduct of rulings of Issur VeHeter third order letters 25 and 26 what he wrote on this; and see below s.k. 3:`,
    '2#_': `Decree. See in responsum Noda BiYehudah second series Yoreh De'ah siman 51: whether also when forbidden from a rabbinic issur the law is thus; and he wrote there that by law it is permitted l'chatchila, for it is a decree upon a decree; only one should not be lenient in this since it is not found in the poskim that they write this law — see there; and in truth it is found thus in Taz below siman 137 s.k. 7 who wrote explicitly that it is permitted — see there (but Taz according to his view in siman 90 s.k. 12 wondered at Issur VeHeter who forbids pickling in milk of a non-kosher slaughter — for it is a decree upon a decree, and Nachalat Binyamin already challenged him there from Tosafot beginning of Kol HaBasar — see there; and this is the view of Issur VeHeter that Taz brought there, also what he brought in siman 137 — see there) [and in responsum Chavot Tzvi end of seif 75 it is explained explicitly that in such a case we decree a decree upon a decree, for in a common and regular matter we decree g'l'g from that which they placed on the head of the cane or on the head of the spindle, etc. — see there; and see below s.k. 3 in the name of responsum Brit Avraham] and at first glance there is clear proof that one should not permit a pot that is ben yomo from a rabbinic issur from the incident of R. Ami in Chullin daf 111, and blood that was salted is only rabbinic — and why did he break it, leave it until tomorrow and it would be permitted? (and see in Shach above siman 23 s.k. 19 and in siman 57 s.k. 47 and in responsum Or Zarua Panim Me'irim vol. 1 siman 23 — investigate) or it is forbidden; and in Ran there it is explained explicitly thus, who wrote on this incident: from here one may prove that nat bar noten ta'am is forbidden l'chatchila — for if not, leave it until tomorrow — end of his words; and therefore he intended this, that even from a rabbinic issur a ben yomo pot is forbidden, for otherwise what does it teach us — is not the Gemara arranged at the end of Avodah Zarah? However one may distinguish between whether the issur became known before it became ben yomo or afterward — and see what I wrote below end of seif 110 in the laws of safek sefeika letter 19 — requires study on this:`,
    '3#_': `There are those who say regarding sharp items. See in responsum Chacham Tzvi siman 75 who ruled to permit if one cooked on Pesach a sharp food in a chametz pot that passed twelve months, for even though we forbid on Pesach noten ta'am lifgam, and likewise in a sharp item noten ta'am lifgam does not help — nevertheless after twelve months there is no taste at all, as they said regarding jugs of libation wine that after twelve months they are permitted (as below end of seif 135); and even though in other issurim we do not find one who is lenient to cook l'chatchila in utensils that passed twelve months upon them — one may distinguish that with libation wine, within twelve months even b'dieved is forbidden — the matter is very proven — therefore l'chatchila is permitted after twelve months; but in other issurim, whatever is ben yomo is permitted b'dieved — it is easy to err if we permit after twelve months l'chatchila lest they permit also within twelve months l'chatchila; and he wrote that accordingly with chametz utensils, according to what we rule that with chametz nat bar noten ta'am is forbidden, and within twelve months even b'dieved is forbidden — automatically the leniency emerges that after twelve months l'chatchila is permitted like libation wine; and nevertheless his heart was not filled to permit to cook l'chatchila, and the gaon Maharatz Katz agreed with him in siman 76 — see there; and see in responsum Or Zarua Panim Me'irim vol. 1 siman 23 who disagreed with him; and see in Chavot Tzvi siman 80 what he answered to Or Zarua and strengthened his words; and see Pri Megadim in siman 103 who wrote that in cold, such as through pickling, Pri Megadim also agrees it is permitted and aging helps, for they disagree in hot — see there further; and in responsum Chayim Sha'al siman 38 he wrote that Rashba's words in responsum siman 475 are not like Chavot Tzvi; and he wrote that an incident came to his hand: a Jew was sent a gift of fine porcelain utensils and it was not discernible if a non-Jew had already used them, and twelve years passed; and he wrote that at first glance one should not permit l'chatchila as Chavot Tzvi himself wrote, and also Rashba disagrees; but here where there is safek sefeika there is room to be lenient, and still requires resolution — end of his words; and see in responsum Turei Chayim siman 491 who wrote on this that if it is possible to kasher them three times one should do so, and if they are utensils whose manner is not to use in boiling — there is room to be lenient — see there; and see in responsum Or HaGolah Rabbi Akiva Eiger siman 43 who was asked on similar case: one who bought an earthenware utensil from a non-Jew that are older than twelve months and there is great loss if he must sell them to a non-Jew — the questioner inclined to be lenient on the basis of the responsum of Chavot Tzvi mentioned above, and he answered him that there is no permission here, for behold Chavot Tzvi wrote himself that in other issurim it is forbidden l'chatchila, and also with chametz utensils he did not decide to be lenient, and also because Rashba's responsum disagrees, and also there is no room to be lenient on the basis of what Minchat Yehudah wrote that in case of great loss one may permit in all cases to use after from time to time, for it is b'dieved as Ran wrote perek kli sheni, and so is the view of Maharalbach siman 121 — this is not so, for behold in Maharalbach there it is proven that specifically where the utensil itself is the great loss like the case of the portable oven one may be lenient, but where each utensil individually is not the great loss, only through their combination — one should not be lenient (and see there what he wrote to distinguish between this and what he wrote in his name end of seif 41); and if so in our case where the great loss is only through having many utensils it is not called b'dieved — see there; and it appears where the utensil itself is great loss like an oven, one may somewhat be lenient according to Maharalbach — requires study on this because of the words of Hagahot Asheri mentioned above that it is explained that even where it is certainly b'dieved it is forbidden; and see in responsum Shvut Yaakov siman 21 who raised to permit b'dieved even after two months — see there; and see in responsum Shemen Rokeach part 1 siman 2 that even after twelve months it is difficult to rely on Chavot Tzvi and to be lenient; but for a pot cover where otherwise it is a stringency without taste there is room to be lenient after twelve months, and it is possible even after two months as the words of Shvut Yaakov — see there; and see further in responsum Beit Avraham part Yoreh De'ah siman 8 letter 4 who wrote on what Chavot Tzvi wrote that in other issurim it is forbidden l'chatchila after twelve months — for we decree lest within twelve months — one may say this is regarding d'oraisa issur, but if the issur itself is rabbinic there is room to incline to permit even l'chatchila; and nevertheless it is difficult to rely on this alone unless there is another leniency — see there further that these twelve months we do not require complete ones, only lunar months, each month twenty-nine days:`,
    '4#_': `That are not ben yomo. And see in responsum Mayim Rabim siman 17: one sage wrote on the matter mentioned there, and these are his words: since it is known that all the gentiles who sell are poor and do not have another utensil to cook their dish — if so these utensils do not enter the category of stam utensils of a non-Jew — end of his words; and see there in siman 11 what he challenged against the sage; and in my humble opinion this is comparable to what was written in the name of Noda BiYehudah in Vienna, Shalien — therefore the law is with the sage:`,
    '5#_': `To say to a non-Jew "cook for me." See in Sefer Lechem She'omrim: he was uncertain if the non-Jew does this on his own for the Jew alone, or for the people of the city to sell to them and most of the city is Jewish — and all the more if the Jew knows he did it for him, for in such a case it is possible it is also forbidden — see there:`,
    '6#_': `So too stam utensils of ours. Avodat HaTaharah siman 104; and see in responsum Be'er Sheva siman 30 who wrote: even though they equated them, the poskim, regarding that they are in a presumption of not being ben yomo — nevertheless there is a great difference between them, for we do not say stam utensils of ours are not ben yomo except when the owner of the utensil is not before us to ask him; but with a utensil of a non-Jew, even though the non-Jew is before us, one need not ask, because he is not believed and his word neither raises nor lowers — see there:`,
    '7#_': `We follow the lenient view. And for this reason he ruled in responsum Shvut Yaakov vol. 1 siman 47: regarding one who found knives in the Jewish street where gentiles are commonly found, since it is a thoroughfare — the knives are permitted without any kashering, and we do not say here that it deteriorated through falling — see there; and see in responsum Chavot Yair siman 228 from this:`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '2#_': `And even so the Sages forbade, etc. And in Mordechai they were lenient, as above siman 108 seif 3:`,
    '4#_': `It is considered as if it was cooked again, etc. The reason is that with meat and milk we say the piece itself becomes nevelah — therefore these waters become nevelah entirely through absorption of meat-and-milk issur that is already in the pot, and it gave taste in the water; but in other issurim we do not say the piece itself becomes nevelah, as he wrote siman 103:`,
    '5#_': `Small issur, etc. In siman 99 seif 7 we explained that for the halachah we do not hold thus — see there:`,
    '6#א': `Therefore if one transgressed and used. Explanation: permitted food that is not sharp; but if it is a sharp food — certainly forbidden, as above seif 3; and the reason they establish utensils of a non-Jew as not ben yomo — Tur wrote that it is because of safek sefeika: a doubt whether they used it today, and even if they used it today, lest they used it with something that spoils; and it is written in Tur: and according to this the law is likewise with our utensils, that there is safek sefeika — we rely to be lenient — end of his words; and so it is written in Shulchan Aruch nearby seif 7; and it is written in Darkei Moshe in Hagahot ShaDa: it is written that with a Jew's pot there is remedy through asking, and if he does not know — penalize him — end of his words; and it appears to me that penalty applies only to that Jew whose pot it is and he should know; but for another penalty does not apply, as above siman 99 — end of his words, Darkei Moshe; and Rashal wrote in chapter Kol HaBasar siman 65: it appears to me that even though stam utensils of a non-Jew are not ben yomo, and presumably one need not ask whether it is ben yomo or cooked in it something that does not spoil — nevertheless if one asked them and they told him it is ben yomo, one should also ask them what was cooked in it today, and if it is something that does not spoil, or it is a matter where he cannot know from them what was cooked in it — one should not permit, since there is no safek sefeika here; and even though we are not concerned for the words of the non-Jew neither to forbid nor to permit — that is regarding that thing that was already in a presumption of kashrut; but here, since the presumption that stam utensils of gentiles are absorbed with issur has already been impaired, only through safek sefeika it came to permit — therefore we rely on his word; and furthermore this that we do not rely on the words of a non-Jew is where he comes to improve his merchandise that he says they are of orlah, as is stated in the Gemara — end of his words; and it does not appear thus to me, for what he wrote that after one asked him and he told him it is ben yomo — there is no safek sefeika here; behold certainly there is safek sefeika here: a doubt lest he is lying in what he says that he used it today, for stam a non-Jew is presumed to lie, as is found in chapter Chazakat HaBatim regarding one who was forced for his donkey — from what is written "whose mouth speaks vanity," etc.; and furthermore what he wrote that here the presumption of kashrut of the utensil was impaired — why do you see that we follow the presumption of the utensil, go after the presumption of the dish that cooks in it that has a presumption of kashrut — and this is exactly like the sugya in the first chapter of Chullin: establish the knife on its presumption and say it did not spoil; rather establish the animal on its presumption and say it was not slaughtered; and they answer: the knife deteriorated, the animal did not deteriorate — and here likewise exactly: the dish has no defect; and what he wrote that we do not rely on the words of the non-Jew — specifically when he intends to improve his merchandise — it is true that at the end of Yevamot it is found thus: a non-Jew who brings fruit to the market and said these fruits are of orlah, etc. — he is not believed neither to forbid nor to permit, only he intended to improve his merchandise; there the Gemara needed this, lest you say since he harms himself and says they are of orlah — certainly he spoke truth — to this he said on the contrary, he intends to improve his merchandise; but otherwise certainly the non-Jew is not believed at all, even to forbid, even where improving merchandise is not relevant — he would say thus, for even a Jew is not believed to forbid his fellow's property as long as it is not in his hand — and so it is found in Mordechai perek R. Yishmael regarding a maidservant who said the wine that was found she drew it — and he wrote, and these are his words: and if because of the maidservant's statement there is no concern, for even a Jew is not believed regarding something not in his hand now — end of his words; however it appears to me that if the non-Jew says thus casually of his own accord without any question, and it appears the matters were not directed at a Jew — then one should be stringent and forbid the dish of a Jew who cooks in it; and proof for this from what he wrote in siman 113 seif 2, when the non-Jew says casually that a Jew commanded him to cut the blemish that is forbidden; and one may further discuss this as he ruled in siman 16, that if the non-Jew is believed to him it is forbidden — so too one may discuss here even when he does not speak casually:`,
    '6#ב': `But if he heated water. See siman 108 where I wrote permission to buy cooked nuts from a non-Jew:`,
    '7#_': `So too stam utensils of ours. I explained it nearby:`,
    '9#א': `But for an hour one need not be concerned. This is from Mordechai's words that he copied nearby, who wrote thus regarding knives: specifically if he left them with the non-Jew half a day or more, but for an hour — no — end of his words; and it is difficult, for in siman 136 Tur wrote even if they did not stand there only one hour — kashering is required; and in Issur VeHeter general rule 33 he distinguishes: if one sends from outside the city — then the non-Jew is not trembling — forbidden even for an hour; but from house to house he trembles for an hour and it is permitted even l'chatchila, unless it stood there half a day; and Maharach wrote: there is a dispute here — one who teaches this did not teach that; and in my humble opinion it appears that a decree for half a day or for an hour is not written in the Gemara that a distinction was made between them; rather the essence is that everything is according to its context: in a matter whose manner is to use frequently, even occasionally, such as a wine utensil that a person is apt to drink at any hour — one should be concerned lest in the short time it was with the non-Jew he drank wine from it — therefore they were stringent in siman 136 even for an hour; unlike a utensil in which they cook, or an eating knife, whose manner is not to use except at mealtime, and a person's manner is not to eat except a fixed measure in a day — therefore one should not be stringent except when it stood, like half a day; and some say not half a day but a large measure that one can estimate that in that time he used it for eating; and this is a matter learned from its context — Tur did not write to be stringent for an hour except in siman 136 that deals with a wine utensil — so it appears to me for practical halachah:`,
    '9#ב': `And all this when one wishes to use, etc. This is from Mordechai's words in perek Ein Ma'amidin, and these are his words: stam utensils of gentiles are not ben yomo; and one may say it is a d'oraisa safek; one may say it is safek sefeika — a doubt whether they used it today, and if you say they used — lest with something that spoils they used; and according to this, when they bring knives to sharpen in the craftsman's house and return them the same day — there is not this reason of safek sefeika, because the same day he returned them, and there is no room to say a doubt lest he did not use at all, for certainly he is accustomed to use since no Jew stands over him — therefore it requires hag'alah; and so Rabbeinu Tam — one must go for sharpening and he was stringent with them more than utensils of a non-Jew themselves; and specifically when he left them half a day in the house of the non-Jew or more, but for an hour — no; however those knives that gentile servants carry in a Jew's house here and there — those one may permit for the reason of safek sefeika like utensils of a non-Jew, and it is proper to be stringent; and they asked Rav R' Shimshon of Bunberg only about a Jew who baked in the house of the non-Jew and forgot the cauldron that they heated hot water in the house of the non-Jew until the next day — and he answered: even though we learned, etc. — all the more with the non-Jew one may say he used them; however one may say establish the cauldron on its presumption and say it did not use issur, and even if it used it would be spoiled that is not ben yomo, and stam utensils of gentiles we establish them thus, as Rabbeinu Tam's words — end of his words; and Issur VeHeter general rule 33 wrote: Mordechai's words here where he was stringent at first with knives more than a non-Jew's cooking — presumably he speaks b'dieved, that if one cut boiling meat with that knife it is as if it is a non-Jew's ben yomo utensil — for otherwise what difference is there, behold even a utensil of a non-Jew requires hag'alah l'chatchila, and if one forgot an earthenware bowl at home — break it — end of his words; it appears that even where there is safek sefeika in a Jew's utensil that he left in a non-Jew's house it is forbidden even b'dieved; and so appears Mordechai's intent from that he said "however those knives," etc. — one may permit because of safek sefeika — it appears that above safek sefeika does not help for Rabbeinu Tam's stringency; and it is difficult: why did Rabbeinu Tam actually be stringent where there is safek sefeika, for in the entire Torah safek sefeika helps; and in Turei Chayim general rule 59 he wrote that Rabbeinu Tam was stringent l'chatchila here more than l'chatchila with a non-Jew's utensil, but b'dieved permitted — and it is not clear at all, since both are forbidden l'chatchila, what stringency in one more than its fellow; and furthermore we already proved from Mordechai that in Rabbeinu Tam's stringency safek sefeika does not help at all even b'dieved — he did not permit b'dieved except with gentile servants in a Jew's house specifically; and it appears to me: all safek sefeika that is permitted b'dieved is after safek sefeika came to be; but this Jew who intentionally leaves his knife in a non-Jew's house and relies that there will be safek sefeika here — one should be stringent; and accordingly if one forgot his knife in a non-Jew's house it is permitted where there is safek sefeika; and accordingly Mordechai's words afterward in the name of R' Shimshon of Bunberg to permit the cauldron for the reason of safek sefeika is resolved; and at first glance it contradicts what he wrote first in the name of Rabbeinu Tam to be stringent even with safek sefeika; and Beit Yosef at the end of this siman wrote that R' Shimshon permits since he returned them the next day — there is safek sefeika, and above he returned them the same day and there is only one doubt — and it is impossible to say this, since we already proved that even with safek sefeika Rabbeinu Tam is stringent; and Maharach wrote that R' Shimshon disagrees with the earlier case — and it does not appear to me thus, for Mordechai's language does not imply dispute, rather according to what I wrote it works out: Rabbeinu Tam was stringent only when placing intentionally, but when forgetting it is like a utensil of a non-Jew and permitted with safek sefeika; and R' Shimshon deals with forgetting and not like Issur VeHeter who is stringent even when forgetting; but nevertheless one may say that even R' Shimshon did not permit the cauldron except if one cooked in it b'dieved afterward — until here it appears to me that even where there is safek sefeika l'chatchila kashering is required since it is possible here; but with earthenware that requires breaking — here it would be l'chatchila permitted, for it is b'dieved; and we find such a distinction in perek Chomer BaKodesh (daf 22) that he distinguishes between earthenware and all other utensils; and even though it is according to Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel are lenient in all utensils — nevertheless Beit Shammai's reasoning is true where one should be stringent, such as here; and we say not to be stringent entirely; and further proof from what Tur wrote siman 142: a new oven that they lit with wood of issur — permitted l'chatchila to bake in it, etc., for it is considered b'dieved since it must be dismantled — end of his words; and so too here — so it appears to me for practical halachah; and all the above is where there is safek sefeika; but where there is no safek sefeika, such as that the non-Jew returned them the same day — forbidden by law, for there is only one doubt, as Mordechai wrote: there is no room to say lest he did not use, and if you say he used — lest with something that spoils, for every stam utensil that is used — and this is a regular doubt that is not considered a doubt at all, as Teshuvot HaRosh wrote siman 171 regarding wormy wheat that every regular doubt is not considered a doubt at all; and even though in siman 84 seif 9 I wrote that even a regular doubt is considered safek sefeika there — nevertheless here it is more regular, since Mordechai did not want to count it as safek sefeika; and if so it is forbidden here b'dieved when returning them the same day; and Beit Yosef in this siman wrote in the name of Orach Chayim in the name of Ra'ah: our bowls that stood in the craftsman's house without a siman — leave it from time to time and it is permitted — end of his words; from that he does not distinguish how long they stood in the craftsman's house and when he returned them — it implies in all cases he speaks that one leaves it and it is permitted, and he disagrees with Mordechai above; and it appears we hold like Mordechai: first, Mordechai is a famous later decisor and in most of his matters we practice accordingly; and furthermore Ra'ah's words are puzzling, for behold with a utensil of a non-Jew it is also permitted b'dieved specifically, and we do not say leave them because of a decree lest one use it today — here too likewise; and in Turei Chayim general rule 59 he wrote that Ra'ah deals where he did not return them the same day — there is safek sefeika — then standing helps; and it appears that nevertheless Ra'ah's words are not for halachah, for if it deals with a matter where Rabbeinu Tam's stringency applies who is stringent even with safek sefeika with a Jew's utensil that he left with a non-Jew — certainly Ra'ah's words are void against Rabbeinu Tam's words; and if it deals with a matter where Rabbeinu Tam's stringency does not apply — that is like R' Shimshon's incident that we mentioned above — then standing is not needed, and like R' Shimshon's ruling, and one should not permit on the basis of what Beit Yosef brings in siman 136 in the name of Ran in the name of Ra'ah and Rav Ramban regarding a wine utensil that b'dieved a Jew's utensil that was in a non-Jew's house is permitted as long as there is doubt whether he used it or not — for the reason that even if he certainly used it he forbids only because of a decree; on the contrary, from there is proof to forbid, for there since the issur is only because of a decree it is like safek sefeika here automatically — where there is only one doubt it is forbidden even b'dieved; and in Turei Chayim there he rules that it appears to him for the halachah that b'dieved it is permitted, for there is no clarity that a non-Jew used it, even though he is accustomed to use — and this is not reasoning at all, as I brought proof above from Teshuvot HaRosh that it is not considered a doubt at all; and one cannot distinguish from his own reasoning to be lenient in what Mordechai explicitly forbids; also what Rama wrote here that there is only one doubt lest he did not use it, etc. — he should not have written thus, for this is not a doubt at all as Mordechai wrote, rather he should have written lest he used with something that spoils — this doubt Mordechai mentioned there and Tur in this siman regarding utensils of a non-Jew — end of his words; it appears there is no leniency b'dieved even for great need, not like Rama wrote nearby and in case of need one may be lenient b'dieved — rather even b'dieved forbidden: General rule: where there is safek sefeika, such as that they did not return it the same day — if one placed intentionally one should forbid the utensil and it requires hag'alah, and earthenware requires breaking; and if one forgot, there is room to permit the utensil, only if it is a utensil that can be kashered one kasheres it, and earthenware is permitted; and so with a wine utensil in siman 136; but where there is no safek sefeika, such as that they returned it the same day — one should forbid even b'dieved by law; and that is: if it is an eating utensil, the issur is only that it should stand with the non-Jew part of the day, a large measure that according to estimation of the mind he used it for eating issur; and if it is a drinking utensil, like that of a wine utensil siman 136 that is forbidden l'chatchila, even for an hour — so it appears to me for practical halachah; and Maharach expanded on this law and raised what he raised, and what appears to me for practical halachah I wrote:`,
    '9#ג': `And in case of need one may be lenient. It was already explained that for the halachah we do not hold thus:`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#_': `Noten ta'am, etc. Chapter 3 of Pesachim and chapter 6 of Nazir and chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah 36a, 35b — baraita: the kafrisin, etc.; baraita: the kosfin, etc.; R. Shimon: this oil, etc.; 39a, 39b and chapter 5 seif 5b, s.k. 7, 8a, 8b, 75b, 76a:`,
    '2#א': `Pot, etc. Chapter 3 of Pesachim and chapter 6 of Nazir and chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah s.k. 7b, 76a — and even rabbinically permitted, as he wrote there 36a, 38b, 39a, 39b, 75b, as mentioned; and as Tosafot wrote in Chullin 8b s.v. haShochet — the Gemara speaks in Avodah Zarah there regarding oil, and likewise regarding honey — why should we be concerned, etc.; nat bar noten ta'am permitted; and the mishnah that forbade in oil — one cannot say he holds nat bar noten ta'am is forbidden, for if so why did they permit in honey? Rather because honey spoils even visibly, unlike oil; and R. Yonah and Beit Din who permitted because it is not ben yomo:`,
    '2#ב': `That is, etc. As he wrote ben yomo — not like Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam there that a night spoils like gestation of form that the food itself is spoiled — for if so why did the Torah require pouring and rinsing in copper utensils and breaking in all earthenware? Behold at the time leftover is made it is spoiled immediately; and one may say it is a decree of the verse — for if so how do we learn from it? The Torah testified regarding all earthenware, etc.; well, if we say from time to time that it is possible leftover will be made since the law of breaking applies to it and its prohibition did not expire — Rosh and Ran (see there); and one need not for this, as he wrote in purging — by Torah law it did not forbid, etc.:`,
    '2#ג': `And even so they forbade, etc. 76a and Tosafot there s.v. miKan, etc.: (Lekut) And even so they forbade, etc. From what he wrote: one who buys a utensil of use, etc., and there: and all of them until, etc. — evidently regarding not ben yomo, and even so specifically b'dieved, but l'chatchila forbidden; and likewise that incident of the bowl that R. Ami broke (Chullin 111b) and pots on Pesach in chapter 2 of Pesachim, etc.; Teshuvot HaRosh 126a (and some say what he wrote in Avodah Zarah 76a "from here onward permit the decree: pot that is not ben yomo because of ben yomo pot" — and see in Sefer HaYashar of Rabbeinu Tam end of seif 481 and in responsum of Rashba siman 498; and in vain he strained on this in responsum of Maharalbach siman 121) (until here):`,
    '2#ד': `Whether, etc. Avodah Zarah there:`,
    '2#ה': `And whether, etc. From what Pesachim wrote 30a: pots on Pesach, etc. (and see what our rabbi wrote in Orach Chayim siman 347 seif 10):`,
    '3#א': `There are those who say, etc. See below siman 150 seif 5 — there are those who disagree on this and permit even if he did not rinse; and their proof is from what he wrote there 39a in a kort of chalitit, and even though the master said, etc., and even though stam a knife is not well scraped, as Rashi wrote in Chullin 112a s.v. kishut — but a knife sometimes, etc., and as I wrote above siman 95 seif 1 — see there; and further proof from what he wrote there 75b: and all of them that were used in them, etc.; one taught one, etc.; and therefore it is not actual immersion — for if so what is the reason of those who forbid? Behold there is no noten ta'am at all — rather this is rinsing; for one who says nat bar noten ta'am is permitted even if he did not rinse — permitted; and this contradicts the first proof of chalitit that is sold in a shop and the shopkeeper scrapes his utensils — and that which he did not immerse — he immersed actually, as Tosafot wrote there s.v. veKulan, etc.; and so too Raavad; but Rambam explained in chapter 17 he rinsed, and here stam like the first reasoning there, and so in several places: (Lekut) There are those who say, etc. From what he wrote (in Chullin) 111b: radish that one cut, etc.; and Rashi explained there in the first language because of the grease stuck to the knife, and even though it is not ben yomo, and as I wrote above siman 96; and Rashba agreed to this explanation — for the second explanation it is difficult why dragging on kishut is needed, and as mentioned (in Lekut as I wrote above siman 96 s.k. 15); and what he wrote in Avodah Zarah 75b: and all of them, etc. — Raavad explained: what he wrote "until he immerses" — actual immersion they spoke of, not rinsing; for rinsing for everyone is forbidden; and see end of seif 120; and Ramban disagrees and permits even without rinsing; and Avodat HaTaharah 125b, 126a (until here): (Lekut) There are those who say, etc. Tosafot Avodah Zarah seif 9a s.v. amar; or there, etc.; but in Chullin there the first reading is known, etc. (until here):`,
    '3#ב': `And nevertheless, etc. Not like Rashal who wrote that since there is substance visibly, then the absorbed also is noten ta'am for improvement:`,
    '3#ג': `There are those who say regarding sharp items, etc. As he wrote regarding a kort of chalitit, and even though, etc.; and likewise regarding radish Chullin 112a — according to the explanation even for ben yomo:`,
    '3#ד': `But the dish is not considered sharp, etc. As he wrote in chapter 6 of Berakhot (36b) and chapter 8 of Yoma: this himeilta, etc.; and they challenged — behold it is a sharp item; and the reading of Semag: in a dry item we do not say its sharpness renders it for improvement; and Turei Chayim challenged him from a kort of chalitit, and likewise in siman 96 seif 3 in hagahah; and Mordechai resolved because they have a designated knife for this; and see what he wrote below; and further reading that one does not cut it but tears it; and he challenged — behold they cook it in their intestines; and reading that stam utensils of a non-Jew are not ben yomo, as I wrote above in seif 6; and even though it is a sharp item, since they cook it in honey and ginger is small it is not considered a sharp item — and so Semag; and for all the resolutions one must say thus, whether for Semag's reading or Mordechai's second reading; and see what he wrote in seif 6 that Mordechai's first reading is also hidden — see there [and see Mordechai end of chapter 6 of Berakhot and Turei Chayim general rule 61 letter 7]:`,
    '3#ה': `But if the issur was a sharp item, etc. So too Issur VeHeter; and requires study, for in chapter 10 of Terumot we say: all pickled items are permitted except with chasit that is a sharp item, chasit, etc.; and one may distinguish between something that is visible and something absorbed; but from what he wrote in siman 105 seif 1 in hagahah "and all the more if," etc. — it does not appear thus; and further Issur VeHeter wrote: and likewise if it is cold we do not say it emits because of the sharp item inside — meaning, even though conversely it emits even in cold like chalitit and radish; and there is proof for his words from what he wrote in perek kli sheni 30b: all utensils, etc.; and the Rif's reading: chametz was used cold, matzah is used cold — and Maggid Mishneh explained: even though he used only in cold, nevertheless it absorbed like when used in hot, because chametz is sharp and strong like stam yeinam — that all whose beginning of use is in the hand of a non-Jew is forbidden, as he wrote in Avodah Zarah 33b: first time, etc.; mania dekunya, etc.; pots of lime, etc.; and inserts for permanence even in metal utensils and the press and the crusher — what he wrote at the end of Avodah Zarah that all are forbidden in cold; and the same applies to chametz that absorbs in cold — everything that has sharpness absorbs even in cold, and it is somewhat comparable to its use in hot, as he wrote regarding chalitit and radish; and if one uses it hot it emits emission, but in cold permitted — evidently it does not absorb in cold; and even though if used hot it is forbidden even to use in cold — he wrote there because of a decree lest one use hot, and like every issur whose use is hot that is forbidden l'chatchila even in cold; and likewise a bowl that one salted — forbidden to eat boiling hot, but cold permitted; and even so R. Ami broke it and did not wish to use it cold; and so one may learn from there the law of the first from what he wrote there (in Pesachim 30a): why leave them, etc.; and Tosafot explained there s.v. leYashneihu: because there are two improvements — nat bar noten ta'am and b'mashehu; and likewise the second reading because of nat bar noten ta'am and permitted because it is called b'dieved, even though it is a sharp item — nevertheless nat bar noten ta'am is permitted because it is not ben yomo; but according to what he wrote in seif 5 there is no proof, and one should not bring proof to forbid nat bar noten ta'am of stam yeinam that all ben yomo is forbidden, as he wrote (in the mishnah of Avodah Zarah 29b): jugs of gentiles and their pitchers and wine of a Jew, etc.; and they said forbidden to make poultices for a donkey lest its jug burst, etc.; there 32a, 74b: see it does not cry out on me, etc.; and even though its use changes year to year — Rosh already wrote in general rule 19 seif 96: specifically something that was cooked is spoiled from time to time, for even a dish that is visible is disqualified through gestation of form, unlike wine that improves with aging, and likewise wine utensils the older they are the better — and see below siman 137; but when examining there is no proof, for some say absorption of utensil from food to absorption of food from utensil; and see what he wrote in siman 105 seif 12 — see there:`,
    '4#א': `Pot, etc. For the water is noten noten ta'am and returns and is absorbed in the pot — Tosafot Avodah Zarah 76a:`,
    '4#ב': `If before, etc. Semak wrote that many disagree on this, since the body of the issur is not here there is no concern; and the view of Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam is that whatever one night passed upon it is not ben yomo; and even though we do not hold like them, nevertheless in such a case one may rely on them, since there are those who are lenient, as mentioned — Beit Yosef:`,
    '4#ג': `Unlike other issurim, etc. According to his view that there is no noten noten ta'am in other issurim; and end of seif 103 — for even the hagahah agrees, since the first are lenient without this, as Semak wrote one may rely on those who hold ein chein nafsho in other issurim:`,
    '5#א': `Issur, etc. As he wrote in Avodah Zarah 33b: a question was raised, etc.; and Tosafot there s.v. shara, etc.; and Rashba wrote: because the utensil's use is cold and its absorption is only a small amount, and they use it abundantly and it is impossible to come, etc.; but wine is forbidden for its issur is b'mashehu; and see there 73a in the mishnah not like Rosh and Ran wrote that the reason is because it spoils, for we say there 73a: wine in water is noten ta'am; rather one may say: some say visible issur to absorbed that spoils in other liquids; and see what he wrote in siman 103 seif 4; but Rashba does not hold to distinguish, and through this Mordechai's challenge in Tosafot Pesachim 30a s.v. leYashneihu is resolved, and one may ask, etc., that even though chametz's use is abundant, nevertheless the chametz inside is small — it is impossible that everything is noten noten ta'am, since it is of heter, as Tosafot wrote at the end of Avodah Zarah; and this is what he wrote like a pot from what was mentioned of Pesachim and a barrel from Avodah Zarah; but Tosafot there follow their view that they do not hold Rashba's reason, and therefore they strained themselves there, and Rashba did not want the first reading there — it is difficult, for there is doubt according to Rava in Avodah Zarah whether Rav holds nat bar noten ta'am is forbidden; and likewise the second reading, for behold jugs — hag'alah is possible; and greater than this: even visible issur b'mashehu they were not concerned in such a case, as he wrote in the last chapter of Terumot: a granary from which terumah wheat was removed, etc.; and likewise a barrel of oil, etc.; and see Rashba siman 222; but Rambam at the end of Hilchot Terumot wrote: a pot in which terumah was cooked — if he rinsed it in water or in wine it is permitted to cook chullin in it; and he wrote in Kesef Mishneh that Rambam wrote in responsum he learned from the mishnah of Terumot there: the pourer sells to the layman — evidently even the small amount visible is permitted, all the more the absorbed; and even rinsing is not needed in cold, only to use in hot requires rinsing; but Raavad already challenged the sugya of Zevachim 96b: Rabbah bar Ulla, etc.; and otherwise from the Torah to be lenient in terumah, and even in terumah in this time d'rabbanan like udder and forbidden fat; and in Yerushalmi there: a pot in which terumah was cooked — purges it three times in hot and enough — R. Ba said: and we do not learn from it for nevelah; R. Yosi: a difficulty before R. Ba — terumah is a capital offense and nevelah is a lav — and you say thus? He said to him: like one who says they accepted the tithes from themselves — meaning they are d'rabbanan; R. Yusti bar Shunam asked before R. Mana: we learned if he bent and squeezed it is terumah — and you say thus? He said to him: here through fire it is purged and emerges — evidently hag'alah is required; but even for everyone hag'alah three times suffices; and he challenged there from "if he bent and squeezed" and the first part did not support him from the pourer sells kedei lekedei, etc. — evidently we are lenient, only not because of leniency, for even in Torah issurim the law is thus because small issur is permitted in abundant use, unlike to kasher that we estimate in all of it in much absorption:`,
    '5#ב': `But if it is a utensil, etc. As he wrote in Pesachim there: a decree lest he come, etc.; evidently if its manner is to use what forbids — forbidden for everyone; and some say for jugs, as he wrote there it is merely occasional, and like on first arrival; and we say in perek kli sheni (111b): a bowl that one salted in it, etc.; even though only a k'zayit is forbidden which is a very small amount — and this is what he wrote like a bowl, etc., and even, etc.:`,
    '6#א': `Stam. Avodah Zarah 36a: he holds, etc.; and even though oil in hot is noten ta'am for improvement, as I wrote above seif 2 — see there; and there 38b, 39a, 75b, as mentioned:`,
    '6#ב': `But something, etc. There and there: hot and their oil permitted l'chatchila to purchase:`,
    '6#ג': `And it is possible through, etc. From that himeilta and the first reading in Mordechai that they have designated utensils; and this is what he wrote the perfumers, and the same for other craftsmen, as he wrote there:`,
    '6#ד': `And a ba'al nefesh, etc. As he challenged: if so why did they require hot and their oil because of nat bar noten ta'am, and likewise a kort of chalitit, and even though, etc.; and there with the shopkeeper, as I wrote (in s.k. 7) — for otherwise it would be forbidden because it is not designated:`,
    '7#_': `Just as, etc. For this reason mentioned above that it is safek sefeika; and even though it is possible to ask him, since it is safek sefeika it is permitted, for also with a non-Jew's it is possible through an Aramean cover, as Rosh wrote — only one need not; and as I wrote end of seif 110:`,
    '8#_': `A utensil that became forbidden, etc. Tosafot Avodah Zarah 73b s.v. tevel, etc.; and one may ask, etc.; and Rashba explained: there it deals with one who has livelihood from elsewhere and has no loss; and that of Avodah Zarah deals with one who does not have, etc.; and as he wrote in chapter 3 of Challah: and he will buy from elsewhere he will lose; and likewise if he separates from all of it he will also lose, for he must fix all of them; and so here he must kasher all of them; and so we say in Bava Metzia 53a: and if it is so that he established, etc.; and Tosafot there s.v. leYahadar, etc.: (Lekut) Utensil, etc. In Beit Yosef in the name of Rashba who brings proof from Bava Metzia 53a that they answer there that he did not redeem; and if it is so let him buy tevel from the market and tithe — meaning the Gemara answers d'oraisa and d'rabbanan do not combine, unlike a purchase that is d'rabbanan, as he wrote there chapter 8a, 8b (see there 53a: and let us derive from what, etc.; and see there chapter 8a in Tosafot s.v. tevu'at; and one may ask in Menachot, etc.; and one may say nevertheless, etc.; requires study) (until here):`,
    '9#א': `One should be careful, etc. Avodah Zarah 74b: Rava when, etc.; and Tosafot there s.v. Rava, etc.:`,
    '9#ג': `One must make, etc. There in Tosafot — and it appears that even with one seal it suffices, as Tosafot wrote there; and from Rashi's words there s.v. sachif that three by three is required; but Tosafot explained as Ran wrote in the name of Ramban: sachif on their mouths that it is tightly closed and that is one seal, and aviritzaihu is a small hole from which they draw the wine — he sealed by itself; and as I wrote 29b that everything because of substitution suffices with one seal; and see below siman 136:`,
    '9#ד': `And if one transgressed, etc. As he wrote there: he holds all, etc.; and according to Tur's explanation there that he speaks to forbid b'dieved:`,
    '9#ז': `And in case of need, etc. According to Ran's explanation there that he speaks l'chatchila:`,
    '9#ח': `And l'chatchila, etc. As I wrote above siman 118 seif 12:`,
    '10#_': `Bowls, etc. As mentioned; and if recognizable it is a seal:`,
    '11#א': `Eating utensils, etc. In Shach: (Lekut) Utensils, etc. See below siman 137 seif 6: gentiles, etc.; and in Orach Chayim siman 513 seif 6 in hagahah that he is believed in d'rabbanan; and this is views that disagree among the poskim; and in Beit Yosef there they disagree in the sugya of chapter 10 of Bava Kamma 114 that mesal't is believed in d'rabbanan — do we say thus also regarding a non-Jew or not (until here):`,
    '11#ב': `And some are stringent. For he holds that even in d'rabbanan he is not believed:`,
    '12#_': `(Lekut) There are, etc. As he wrote in Avodah Zarah 33a, and according to Rabbeinu Tam in Tosafot there s.v. kakh, etc.; and as I wrote above siman 135 (until here):`,
  },
};
